.he "execve (2)                   10/20/26                 execve (2)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
execve
- execute a program with an environment
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
C
.in +5
.br
execve(name, argv, envp)
.br
char *name;
.br
char *argv[];
.br
char *envp[];
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Execve
overlays the calling core image with the named file, then transfers
to the beginning of the new core image.  There can be no return from
a successful
.bd
execve:
the calling core image is lost.
.sp
The file is found, checked and loaded exactly as by
.bd
exec
[see
.bd
exec
(2)]: the execute permission bit must be set, the file must be an
ordinary file, and its image must fit in user memory.  Previously
opened files remain open, and ignored signals remain ignored.
Caught signals are reset to their default behavior.
.sp
The difference is the environment.
.bd
Envp
is a list of pointers to strings of the form
.nf
        name=value
.fi
terminated by a null pointer.  The kernel copies the strings along
with the arguments before the old image is released.  When the new
image starts, its stack holds:
.nf
        argc
        argv[0]
        ...
        argv[argc-1]
        -1
        envp[0]
        ...
        envp[n-1]
        0
.fi
The argv terminator is -1, as
.bd
exec
(2) documents, so a program that passes its inherited
.bd
argv
on to
.bd
execv
must set
.bd
argv[argc]
to a null pointer first.  The environment vector follows the
terminator.
The C startup code sets the global
.bd
environ
to the address of
.bd
envp[0],
so the new program can walk that list or call the C library's
.bd
getenv.
A null
.bd
envp,
or one whose first pointer is null, gives the new image an empty
environment.  The older
.bd
exec
call is that case: it passes no environment at all.
.sp
The argument and environment strings are built directly into the new
image's initial stack, in segments of its own, while the old image is
still mapped.  The block is placed at the top of user memory, strings
last, and costs exactly its size; the strings are measured first so
the image gets everything below the block.  The block, the image and
the heap must all fit in 64K.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
RETURNS
.in +5
.br
Any return is an error return, with the value -1 and
.bd
errno
set.  In that case the calling image is not lost and can continue.
The errors are those of
.bd
exec;
an argument or environment list too large for the initial stack page
is reported as E2BIG, and an image that will not fit below that page
as ENOMEM.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
ASSEMBLER
.in +5
.br
.bd
(execve = 56)
.br
.bd
sys; execve; name; argv; envp
.sp
.bd
Argv
and
.bd
Envp
are the addresses of lists of string pointers.  Each list is
terminated by a null pointer.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
